we're going to begin this hour with news  we hope that we would never have to  report to you the death toll from the  covet 19 pandemic has now reached 1  million americans that's according to  the white house president biden will  mark that very sad milestone later today  experts think that the real number is  really much higher and it continues to  grow our lead national correspondent  that would be david beck now has shared  the stories of many of the families  dealing with the laws those who cannot  move on so easily david checked in to  see how they're doing now with the  passage of time  over the past two and a half years we  have interviewed someone from almost  every state in the country who's been  affected by kovid's death grip it's hard  to breathe for him we've told stories of  hundreds of people but we don't often  take the opportunity to circle back and  say  how you doing now  that's what this is about  pamela orlando was a registered nurse  from new jersey and a single mom to two  boys  i remember one of her sons telling me  you know david mom recorded videos of  her downward spiral because she wanted  people to see what the virus was doing  to her i feel horrible  pam died on april 16 2020.  she was only 56.  we checked in on her two sons  her eldest reid had considered a career  in broadcasting but after his mother  died he started working for a medical  staffing company he says  as a way to honor her pam's youngest son  ryan is an 18 year old high school  senior he told us he has dedicated his  studies to his mom and recently he  received a scholarship to go to college  we remember cody lister at one time he  was the youngest person to die of covet  in the state of colorado cody was an  avid baseball player and just an  all-around great athlete  he was 21 when kovitt killed him his  father kevin also fought covet but he  and his wife survived the virus  cody's dad and mom tell us they try and  live lives that would make him proud  they talk to him every day  and share with them the milestones that  he's missed  their new akita puppy his sister  sierra's first prom  she actually plays softball and even  wears her brother's number 13.  [Music]  32 year old jonathan coelho he passed  away on april 22 2020 after 27 days in  the hospital  just a few hours before they placed him  on a ventilator he wrote a note to his  wife katie and she shared it with us  in it he said  it makes me so proud to be your husband  and the father to braden and penny you  could see from start to finish my  husband worshipped us  almost two years later katie tells us  that parenting alone has been a real  challenge especially while grieving but  it is her desire to be strong for their  two children that's what's driving her  forward  she says they have been her brightest  lights since jonathan's death  braden who has cerebral palsy learned to  walk with the aid of a walker but he's  doing it all by himself  penny took her first steps come on  on father's day last year good job  [Laughter]  i remember 33 year old angel moseley  of new orleans now hers was a  breakthrough case of covid she actually  contracted the disease and died despite  being fully vaccinated angel was  hospitalized on a friday  she died two days later july 25th 2021  we flew down to new orleans shortly  after she passed away and sat with her  mother at the family's church if she  were here right now what would she want  you to say  keep our business going because that was  her baby that was her dream  i am happy to report to you that  angelle's legacy not only lives on  but her request is being honored that's  a beautiful color on gel's clothing  store the brave boutique which  specializes in plus size clothing for  women is still open and it's being run  by her mom  and her friends  just two hours west of new orleans is  where david lemare lived in lafayette  he had just graduated from high school  when he died due to covet he and his  brother jacob were actually both  hospitalized in the same icu with covet  19 related pneumonia  the brother survived david fought for  weeks their father mark who is a doctor  spent so many days by david's bedside  praying  and watching his son's slow  deterioration i learned a lot about my  son  about how compassionate he was and how  deep his faith was david died july 31st  2020  his loved ones commemorated his passing  with a balloon release one year later  david had a love of photography  especially landscapes  his dad told us that whenever he sees a  sunset it gives him a good feeling that  his son is watching over them  alfredo and susanna pavitau of new  jersey were married for 44 years they  both contracted covid and they died  within four days of each other back in  march 2020. now they had been on kovit's  front lines she was a nurse he was a  medical transporter  by  their  daughter  gets harder for me we checked in on  cheryl and she told us that osha  investigated the hospital where her  parents died accusing the hospital of  failing to protect employees from  exposure to coronavirus now cheryl had  had concerns that her father was not  provided with the proper ppe nor was he  trained on how to use it  cheryl tells us after all of this she  has become a certified health inspector  and  she wants to be an osha investigator  herself  finally she told us it was her mother's  wish that she would find love  she has  meet uber  i believe my husband was sent to me from  my parents she told us  [Music]  you probably have noticed the chairs  that i'm surrounded by  they are empty  and that is meant to honor those who we  didn't name  those whose names and stories  may never be known publicly  may their memories be a blessing  david we certainly did notice the chairs  and we all thought that's exactly what  you were doing i remember when we used  to do these stories once a week we'd go  through and remember that remember the  people that did not survive it just it's  a reminder again guys about cova that it  it doesn't care about your age your race  your gender and i think it's so  important we keep throwing around this  number one million people as you've  shown us be fine those one million  people are real names of people who were  loved and dearly missed as we said they  have stories yes i'll never forget it's  so hard to wrap your head around that a  number of that size and david i mean i  commend you for doing such a tremendous  job in helping us engage with those  stories as representative of that bigger  number and then the empty chairs is just  such a powerful metaphor for what this  country has lost  tony i just want to say to them to the  families we've spoken to thank you for  allowing us to tell your stories and to  those we haven't  we are thinking of you this morning this  was a tribute to all of you  exactly right and i think we need to  keep reminding people this is what kova  does it tears people apart and leaves  behind tremendous tremendous pain for  sure david thank you very much  you better

